**Answer: Yes, the solution is unique.**

**Proof.**

Write the equation as
$$w'' + k^2 w = -u(x)w, \qquad k>0.$$
The free operator $L_0 = \tfrac{d^2}{dx^2}+k^2$ has, for $y>x$, the Green's kernel
$$G(x,y)=\frac{\sin k(y-x)}{k}, \qquad G(x,y)=0\ \text{for}\ y<x,$$
which is the unique kernel satisfying $(\partial_x^2+k^2)G(\cdot,y)=\delta(\cdot-y)$ with the *incoming* (decaying oscillatory) boundary condition at $+\infty$, i.e. $G(x,y)\sim \frac{1}{2ik}e^{-ikx}\cdot(\text{const})$ as $x\to+\infty$ for fixed $y$. Consequently any solution of the inhomogeneous equation that is asymptotic to $e^{-ikx}$ at $+\infty$ satisfies the Volterra integral equation
$$w(x)=e^{-ikx}-\int_x^\infty \frac{\sin k(y-x)}{k}\,u(y)\,w(y)\,dy. \tag{1}$$
(One verifies directly by differentiating twice that (1) solves $w''+(k^2+u)w=0$; conversely, integrating the ODE against $G$ from $x$ to $+\infty$ and imposing $w(\infty)\sim e^{-ikx}$ yields (1).)

**Uniqueness.** Suppose $w_1,w_2$ both solve the ODE and satisfy $\lim_{x\to\infty}w_j(x)e^{ikx}=1$. Set $v=w_1-w_2$. Then $v$ solves the homogeneous ODE $v''+(k^2+u)v=0$ with
$$\lim_{x\to\infty} v(x)e^{ikx}=0, \tag{2}$$
and, subtracting (1) for $w_1$ and $w_2$,
$$v(x)=-\int_x^\infty \frac{\sin k(y-x)}{k}\,u(y)\,v(y)\,dy. \tag{3}$$

Define $g(x):=v(x)e^{ikx}$. By (2), $g(x)\to 0$ as $x\to\infty$; in particular $g$ is bounded on $[R,\infty)$ for large $R$. Since $|v(y)|=|g(y)|$ (because $|e^{iky}|=1$), (3) gives
$$|g(x)|\le \frac1k\int_x^\infty |u(y)|\,|g(y)|\,dy. \tag{4}$$

Introduce
$$G(x):=\int_x^\infty |u(y)|\,|g(y)|\,dy\ge 0.$$
Because $u\in L^1(\mathbb R)\subset L^1([R,\infty))$ and $g$ is bounded, $G(x)\to 0$ as $x\to\infty$, and $G$ is absolutely continuous with
$$G'(x)=-|u(x)|\,|g(x)|.$$
Using (4), $|g(x)|\le G(x)/k$, hence
$$G'(x)=-|u(x)|\,|g(x)|\ge -\frac{|u(x)|}{k}\,G(x).$$
Therefore
$$\frac{d}{dx}\Bigl[G(x)\,e^{-\frac1k\int_x^\infty |u(s)|\,ds}\Bigr]\ge 0.$$
Wait — let me instead integrate the differential inequality directly. From $G'/G\ge -|u|/k$ (on $\{G>0\}$), for any $X>x$,
$$\log G(X)-\log G(x)\ge -\frac1k\int_x^X |u(s)|\,ds,$$
so
$$G(X)\ge G(x)\,\exp\!\Bigl(-\frac1k\int_x^X |u(s)|\,ds\Bigr)\ge G(x)\,\exp\!\Bigl(-\frac1k\|u\|_{L^1}\Bigr)>0$$
whenever $G(x)>0$. Letting $X\to\infty$ gives $G(X)\to 0$ on the left while the right-hand side stays bounded below by a strictly positive constant — a contradiction. Hence $G(x)=0$ for every $x$.

Since $G\equiv 0$ and $|u|\ge 0$, $|g|\ge 0$ are continuous, (4) forces $g\equiv 0$, i.e. $v\equiv 0$. Thus $w_1=w_2$.

**Remark on the role of $u\in L^1_1$.** The argument above uses only $u\in L^1(\mathbb R)$ (the weight $(1+|x|)$ is needed for *existence* and for the regularity of the Jost solution / analyticity in $k$, but uniqueness of the solution satisfying the single Jost-type condition at $+\infty$ follows from the $L^1$ decay alone via the Volterra–Gronwall estimate). The second-order ODE has a two-dimensional solution space; the single asymptotic condition $\lim_{x\to\infty}w(x)e^{ikx}=1$ removes one degree of freedom (the coefficient of $e^{+ikx}$ is forced to $0$), and the Volterra structure of (1) — integrating only over $[x,\infty)$ with an $L^1$ kernel — removes the remaining freedom, pinning down $w$ uniquely.

Therefore the solution $w$ is **unique**.

### PROOF COMPLETE
